// @test: verify-pass
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: refinement, smt, bitwise, translation, verification
// @timeout: 30000

/// `&`, `|` and `^` on integers are BITWISE, and the verifier can reason
/// about them.
///
/// It could not. The translator's integer branch listed them beside the
/// logical operators and answered
///
///     operator ^ requires boolean operands, got integers
///
/// which describes a language that does not exist — Verum's logical pair
/// is `&&` and `||`. The cost was not a bad message. A body containing
/// one failed to translate at all, so `result` was never bound to it and
/// every claim about the function became unprovable. Measured, with no
/// array and no loop in sight:
///
///     fn f(n: Int) -> Int ensures result == 0
///     { let mut d = 0; d = d | (n ^ n); d }
///
///     ✗ Failed, counterexample `d = 0, result = 2`
///
/// `result = 2` is the tell: `n ^ n` is zero for every n, so a bound
/// `result` could not have been 2. It was not bound at all.
///
/// This is the second of three layers under the showcase application's
/// hash theorems, which all attribute themselves to loops. The first was
/// the assignment (T0954); this is the operator; the loop is the third
/// and has not been reached yet. A version of the byte comparison
/// unrolled BY HAND, with no loop anywhere, still failed here.
///
/// WIDTH. The translation goes through a 64-bit bitvector and back, and
/// that is exact for every integer type Verum has, not an approximation
/// for `Int`. For AND/OR/XOR the sign-extension of the narrow result
/// equals the wide result of the sign-extended operands: the high bits of
/// a sign-extended `a` are all `sign(a)`, so the high bits of the wide
/// answer are `sign(a) op sign(b)` — which is the sign bit of the narrow
/// answer. Shifts and `~` do not have that property and are refused with
/// that as the stated reason.
///
/// The soundness pole is `bitwise_operators_stay_honest.vr`.
///
/// Task: T0956.

@verify(formal)
public fn xor_with_itself_is_zero(n: Int) -> Int
    ensures result == 0
{
    n ^ n
}

// THE KNOWN LIMIT, measured rather than assumed. `n & n == n` and
// `n | 0 == n` are NOT stated here, and the reason is worth more than the
// two lines they would occupy.
//
// The solver models Verum's `Int` as an UNBOUNDED mathematical integer,
// while the language's `Int` is 64 bits. The bitvector round-trip is exact
// inside 64 bits and lossy outside it, so an identity that returns the
// operand — `n & n`, `n | 0` — is not valid for the unbounded `n` the
// solver is reasoning about, and it is correctly refused. Identities whose
// answer does not depend on the operand's magnitude (`n ^ n == 0`,
// `n & 0 == 0`) are unaffected, which is why they are the ones stated.
//
// This is INCOMPLETENESS, not unsoundness: the encoding is exact for
// in-range values and merely permissive outside, so anything proved for
// every integer holds in particular for every `Int`.
//
// Measured: adding `requires n >= 0 && n < 65536` makes both prove — in
// 26s and 55s respectively, against 0.09s for the unbounded-safe
// identities above. So the fix is not to bound them here; it is to give
// the solver the range fact that the type already carries, and the cost of
// bit-blasting says that has to be done carefully. Task: T0961.

@verify(formal)
public fn and_with_zero_is_zero(n: Int) -> Int
    ensures result == 0
{
    n & 0
}

/// The shape the showcase actually writes: an accumulator OR-ed with a
/// XOR difference, which is zero exactly when the two sides agree. This
/// is `equal_narrow` with the loop unrolled by hand.
@verify(formal)
public fn accumulated_difference_of_equals_is_zero(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
    requires a == b
    ensures result == 0
{
    let mut diff = 0;
    diff = diff | (a ^ b);
    diff = diff | (a ^ b);
    diff = diff | (a ^ b);
    diff = diff | (a ^ b);
    diff
}

mount core.prelude.*;

fn main() {
    print("bitwise operators reach the solver");
}
